/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  solvent03 -- THE CONFRONTATION WITH EXPERIMENT (DEV.md 4c C128,
  adjustment 2, step three).

  No `composition {}`: the op SCREENS each declared solvent pure -- one row
  each, Ra and RED against polystyrene's sphere (Hansen 1967, Table 4.1,
  solute G).  Beside the run, constant/experimental/
  hansen1967_table23_polystyrene.csv holds what the same thesis OBSERVED for
  each of these thirteen solvents (Table 2.3, column G, the 1-6 scale of
  page 20: 1 soluble ... 6 no visible effect), with the citation on every
  row.  The op does not read that file: the prediction and the observation
  stay two tables, joined by the reader (the README does it by hand, the
  EduTool draws them side by side, the gate counts the agreement).

  WHAT THE CONFRONTATION IS, said plainly.  Hansen's sphere for this
  polymer was DRAWN THROUGH these very observations (Section 4.4: "the
  centers of the volumes of solubility ... have been determined" from the
  solvents that dissolve each solute), so agreement here shows that the
  transcription and the distance arithmetic reproduce his placement -- it
  is a check of the method on its own training set, not an independent
  prediction.  The thesis itself states the residual: about 2.5 % of the
  solvents beyond R0 + 0.5 dissolve, and about 2.5 % within R0 - 0.5 do
  not (page 39).  Nitroethane (RED 1.39, rated 2 "almost soluble") and
  cyclohexane (RED 1.06, rated 1 "soluble") are that residual, in this set.

  The package is `ideal` because no mixture is asked for: every row is one
  pure solvent and the phase word is `pure`.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name       screen;
        type       hansenMixture;
        target     polystyrene;
        solvents   ( nHexane cyclohexane benzene toluene diethylEther
                     ethylAcetate methylEthylKetone acetone nitroethane
                     nitromethane nButanol ethanol methanol );
        T          298.15 K;
        output     { file "hansen_screen.csv"; }
    }
);
